> Right, but then how do I get anything warmer? I can't change the equations to get more than 2S by making the atmosphere "thicker".  What would change in the equations to turn this into a description of Venus, rather than a 19% warmer ball of rock?
**assistant**:
> A one-layer atmosphere has no notion of *optical thickness*. To represent a thicker IR-absorbing atmosphere, add layers—or use continuous optical depth $\tau$.
> 
> If $N$ perfectly IR-opaque, non-solar-absorbing atmospheric layers each emit equally upward and downward, and absorbed solar flux is $F$, then:
> 
> \[
> \sigma T_{\rm emit}^4 = F
> \]
> 
> at the top of atmosphere, but the surface balance becomes
> 
> \[
> \sigma T_s^4=(N+1)F.
> \]
> 
> Thus,
> 
> \[
> T_s=(N+1)^{1/4}T_{\rm emit}.
> \]
> 
> The one-layer result is simply $N=1$. More layers keep returning radiation downward before a photon ultimately escapes upward. In this toy model, arbitrarily many layers can produce arbitrarily high surface temperature.
> 
> A continuous grey-atmosphere version replaces $N$ with infrared optical depth:
> 
> \[
> T^4(\tau)\approx \frac{3F}{4\sigma}\left(\tau+\frac23\right).
> \]
> 
> Greater greenhouse-gas abundance or pressure raises $\tau$, moving the effective emission level higher. Space still sees only $F$; it sees radiation emitted from the altitude where the atmosphere becomes optically thin. The surface can be much hotter beneath that level.
> 
> For Venus:
> 
> - It absorbs relatively little sunlight because its clouds are bright, giving an effective radiating temperature of only about $230\ \mathrm{K}$.
> - But its $\sim 92$-bar, CO$_2$-dominated atmosphere is extremely optically thick in thermal IR.
> - Radiation to space comes primarily from high cloud/upper-atmosphere levels, around that $\sim230\ \mathrm{K}$ temperature.
> - Beneath the emission level, the deep atmosphere is convective. Temperature increases downward along a roughly adiabatic lapse rate, reaching $\sim735\ \mathrm{K}$ at the surface.
> 
> So the physically useful upgrade is a **radiative–convective column**: radiative transfer determines the emission altitude; convection sets the temperature profile below it. The “extra GHG effect” is not extra net planetary energy, but a larger temperature difference between the cold level emitting to space and the surface.
